Mesoscopic Oxide Double Layer as Electron Specific Contact for Highly Efficient and UV Stable Perovskite Photovoltaics
\(^{1}\)Institut des Sciences et Ingénierie Chimiques, Ecole Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne (EPFL), EPFL–BCH, CH–1015 Lausanne, Switzerland
\(^{2}\)Department of Materials Science and Engineering, Sharif University of Technology, 14588 Tehran, Iran
Abstract
The solar to electric power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has recently reached \(22.7\ \%\) exceeding that of competing thin film photovoltaics and the market leader polycrystalline silicon. Further augmentation of the PCE towards the Shockley-Queisser limit of \(33.5\ \%\) warrants suppression of radiationless carrier recombination by judicious engineering of the interface between the light harvesting perovskite and the charge carrier extraction layers. Here, we introduce a mesoscopic oxide double layer as electron selective contact consisting of a scaffold of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles covered by a thin film of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), either in amorphous \(\left(\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\right)\), crystalline \(\left(\mathrm{c}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\right)\) or nanocrystalline (quantum dot) form \(\left(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}-\mathrm{NC}\right)\). We find that the band gap of \(\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is larger than that of the crystalline (tetragonal) polymorph leading to a corresponding lift in its conduction band edge energy which aligns it perfectly with the conduction band edge of both the triple cation perovskite and the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold. This enables very fast electron extraction from the light perovskite, suppressing the notorious hysteresis in the current-voltage \((J-V)\) curves and retarding nonradiative charge carrier recombination. As a result,
we gain a remarkable \(170\,\mathrm{mV}\) in open circuit photovoltage \((V_{oc})\) by replacing the crystalline \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) by an amorphous phase. Due to the quantum size effect, the band gap of our \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC particles is \(0.4\,\mathrm{eV}\) larger than that of bulk \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) causing their conduction band edge to shift also to a higher energy thereby increasing the \(V_{oc}\). However, for \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC there remains a barrier for electron injection into the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold decreasing the fill factor of the device and lowering the PCE. Introducing the \(\mathrm{a-SnO}_{2}\) coated mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold as electron extraction layer not only increases the \(V_{oc}\) and PEC of the solar cells but render them resistant to UV light which forebodes well for outdoor deployment of these new PSC architectures.
Keywords: Electron specific contact, Amorphous \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), Mesoscopic structure, Efficiency, UV stability.
## Introduction
Organic-inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have been the focus of intensive research during the past few years. So far, compositional engineering and interface modifications have been employed in order to boost the solar to electric power conversion efficiency (PCE) of perovskite solar cell over \(22\%\). Surface modification of the electron extraction layer (EEL) is one of the most effective way to improve the efficiency of perovskite solar cell. \({}^{4-6}\,\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) is the most common EEL for fabrication of PSCs due to its low cost, eco friendliness, ease of fabrication and favorable conduction band alignment with the perovskite. However, the compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (c-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)) layer has a relatively low electron mobility and contains electron traps that retard electron extraction leading to enhanced hysteresis in the current-voltage \((J-V)\) curves and nonradiative charge carrier recombination. To tackle this problem, an additional layer of mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)) consisting of a 3-dimensional array of interconnected \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanocrystals is usually
deposited on top of the \(\mathbf{c}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). However, this EEL structure may bring other risks, such as performance degradation by UV light. Surface modification of \(\mathrm{mp-TiO}_{2}\) scaffold offers an effective way to improve the carrier collection and in turn the photovoltaic metrics of the PSCs.\(^{12-16}\) For instance, lithium treatment of \(\mathrm{mp-TiO}_{2}\) can help to passivate the surface defects of the EEL and improve the charge collection, resulting in a higher \(V_{oc}\) and PCE.\(^{17}\)
\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) films show photocatalytic activity under UV light,\(^{18,19}\) which could affect the perovskite. Therefore, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layers are increasingly replaced by \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films which are photo-catalytically inactive, show very high electron mobility and fast electron extraction.\(^{20-22}\) Methods for depositing \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) based EELs employ e.g. solution processing, atomic layer deposition (ALD), and sputtering technique.\(^{23-26}\) Solution processing has so far yielded the highest PCE of \(21.6\%\) reported by Jiang et al. albeit on a tiny device area of only \(0.0737\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\).\(^{27}\) It appears that the direct deposition of the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) layer onto the FTO glass is not well reproducible due to the roughness of the conductive glass support,\(^{28}\) and there is a risk of infiltration with fluoride ions from the FTO. Therefore, Lee et al. introduced a compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer between the FTO and the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film attaining a maximum PCE of \(19.8\%\).\(^{15}\) Dong et al. employed \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) quantum dots (\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC) mixed with \(\mathrm{mp-TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles as an EEL for PSCs reporting PSC of \(16\%\).\(^{29}\) Jiang et al. utilized only \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NCs as EEL and reported a PSC with certified PCE approaching \(20\%\).\(^{30}\) In addition, Song et al. deposited a thin layer of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) on top of anodized amorphous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (a-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)), yielding a PCE of \(21.1\%\).\(^{28}\) In fact, they concur that using \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) as EEL the photovoltaic performance and stability are increased with decreasing the undesirable hysteresis effect.\(^{31-33}\)
Here we introduce a mesoscopic oxide double layer as an electron selective contact consisting of a scaffold of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles coated by a thin film of amorphous \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (a-\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)) using solution deposition. The band gap of the a-\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) exceeds that of the crystalline tetragonal polymorph,
affording perfect alignment of its conduction band with that of the perovskite light harvester. This accelerates carrier extraction with respect to the bare \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold, avoiding electron trapping by surface states. As a result, the notorious hysteresis in the current-voltage curves of PSCs is suppressed and nonradiative carrier recombination retarded yielding a very high open circuit photovoltage \((V_{oc})\) of \(1.2\;\mathrm{V}\). Results from intensity modulated photovoltage spectroscopy (IMVS) are consistent with these extraordinarily high \(V_{oc}\) values. Importantly, we find that these benefits are specific to \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) and are not observed upon converting it to a crystalline form by high temperature annealing. Apart from enhancing the photovoltaic performance, the \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) coating also prevents UV degradation of the PSC devices based on \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}/\mathsf{m p}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) as EEL retaining \(97\%\) of their initial PCE after \(60\,\mathrm{h}\) exposure to the UV light.
## Result and discussions
Figure S1 shows a schematic of the investigated device structure. It consisted of FTO glass coated by a compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer, a \(150\;\mathrm{nm}\)-thick \(\mathrm{mp}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), a thin layer of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), a \(300\;\mathrm{nm}\)-thick perovskite film, a \(150\;\mathrm{nm}\)-thick spiro-OMeTAD hole transfer layer (HTL), and a \(80\;\mathrm{nm}\) thick gold contact. The mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold was coated by a conformal thin layer of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) in the form of \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\), \(\mathsf{c}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\), or \(\mathrm{NC}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\). Top-view SEM images of the perovskite films deposited on the different EELs are shown in Figure S2. The grain size of the perovskite film on \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) is larger than when is deposited on the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC layer or the bare \(\mathrm{mp}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold. This indicates that there are less nucleation sites on amorphous \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (continuous film) with respect to the other two substrates, resulting in larger grain size for the perovskite film. In order to investigate the uniformity of \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) film on top of \(\mathrm{mp}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) EEL, the elemental mapping of \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}/\mathsf{m p}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) EEL was taken by Energy Dispersive Analysis X-Ray (EDAX) technique, as shown in Figure S3. The result indicates that \(\mathrm{mp}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) EEL is coated by \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) layer with uniform coverage.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films deposited from solution and the NC-colloid are shown in Figure 1a. The data show the spin-coated \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film annealed at \(180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to be amorphous turning fully crystalline upon annealing at \(450\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The reflections match the known pattern of tetragonal \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (JCPDS Card No: 41-1445). The \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC layer shows similar XRD reflections although it exhibits a stronger prevalence of (101) over the (110) and (200) facets. It was deposited onto the mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold by spin coating of a colloidal solution of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanocrystals with a \(3{-}5\;\mathrm{nm}\) particle size (Figure S4), followed by annealing at \(150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
Figures1b and 1c demonstrate the optical properties of the triple cation perovskite films deposited on the different EELs. Panel (b) shows the absorption onset and luminescence peak to be around \(770\ \mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to a band gap of \(1.61\ \mathrm{eV}\). The photoluminance (PL) decay is significantly influenced by the nature of the EEL reflecting its effectiveness in scavenging photogenerated conduction band electrons from the perovskite light harvesters (Figure 1c). The PL lifetimes obtained from exponential fits of the decay curves are \(2.43\ \mathrm{ns}\), \(3.21\ \mathrm{ns}\), and \(1.93\ \mathrm{ns}\) for mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC, and mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), respectively, showing that the amorphous \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) extracts electrons more rapidly from the perovskite than the bare \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold or the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC covered films.
Table 1. Photovoltaic metrics for best performing devices based on mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NCs, and mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) with different scan directions
We fabricated perovskite solar cells endowed as explained in the experimental section. Figure 2a presents the cross-sectional SEM image of a PSC based on mp-TiO\(_2\)/a-SnO\(_2\) as EEL. Current density-voltage \((J-V)\) curves of devices employing the three different EELs under simulated (AM1.5G) solar irradiation are compared in Figure 2b for both backward and forward scanning directions while the PV metrics for best performing devices are listed in Table 1. The PSC based on mp-TiO\(_2\)/a-SnO\(_2\) yields the highest PCE of 20.4% with a \(V_{oc}\) of 1.168 V, \(J_{sc}\) of 22.51, and fill factor \((FF)\) of 77.6%, clearly outperforming the bare mp-TiO\(_2\) and mp-TiO\(_2\)/SnO\(_2\)-NC films. The reference cell using mp-TiO\(_2\) gave a \(J_{sc}\) of 22.21 mA/cm\(^2\), \(V_{oc}\) of 1.098 V, \(FF\) of 78.5%, and PCE of 19.14%.
Note the high \(V_{oc}\) of 1.17 V obtained with mp-TiO\(_2\)/a-SnO\(_2\) as EEL exceeding that of the bare TiO\(_2\) reference by 80-100 mV. This finding is counterintuitive because one would expect SnO\(_2\) to produce a lower \(V_{oc}\) than TiO\(_2\) (anatase) due to the 0.3 eV larger offset of its conduction band edge energy with respect to that of the perovskite. In keeping with this expectation, Dai et. al.\(^{34}\) obtained a \(V_{oc}\) of only 0.7 V with a PSC employing meosporous SnO\(_2\) (mp-SnO\(_2\)) instead of TiO\(_2\) as electron extracting scaffold. Upon covering the mp-SnO\(_2\) film with a thin layer of TiO\(_2\) the \(V_{oc}\) improved to 0.93 V.\(^{35}\) Strikingly, we observe exactly the opposite behavior, i.e. by coating the mp-TiO\(_2\) scaffold with a thin layer of a-SnO\(_2\), we greatly improve the \(V_{oc}\) pushing it to 1.17 V. In
order to rationalize these seemingly contradictory findings, we draw attention to our recent discovery\(^{36}\) that the conduction band edge energy of amorphous \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is lifted by about \(0.5~\mathrm{eV}\) with respect to that of the tetragonal crystalline phase. This shift improves the band alignment with the perovskite allowing for rapid carrier extraction without voltage loss. The amorphous overlayer shows also the lowest hysteresis index, i.e. \(1.1\ \%\) calculated from the formula \(\mathrm{h}\ [\%]=\) \((\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{backward}}-\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{forward}})/\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{backward}})\times100.\) . This again corroborates our finding that electrons are extracted very rapidly by the \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) covered \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold without undergoing any significant trapping at the perovskite/EEL interface.
Figure S5 demonstrates statistical data obtained from batches of 15 PSCs for each of the three EELs. The average values of photovoltaic metrics confirm the trend that the amorphous \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film coating on mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) increases all photovoltaic parameters except \(FF\). In particular, the \(V_{oc}\) of device using mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is greatly increased. However, the presence of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NCs on mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), while increasing the \(V_{oc}\), decreases the \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and especially \(FF\) with respect to the reference cell. This indicates that the structure of the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film plays a key role in device performance. Figure S6 shows the \(J-V\) curve of a PSC using the \(\mathsf{a}-\mathsf{SnO}_{2}\) film on mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) that was subjected to annealing at \(450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) resulting in the formation of a crystalline \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) overlayer. The results prove that the conduction band edge of crystalline \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film is much less favorably aligned with the perovskite layer than that of the amorphous form, resulting in a poorer device performance. Despite similarity of their XRD features, the PV performance of the \(\mathsf{c}-\mathsf{SnO}_{2}/\mathsf{mp}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) EEL is worse than that of the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC, which can be attributed to quantum confinement effects in the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanocrystals,\(^{37}\) changing the band alignment of EEL with respect to perovskite (Figure 3b). Figure 2c shows the stabilized maximum power point delivered from mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC, and mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) PSCs to be 18.89, 16.75, and \(20.27~\mathrm{mW/cm}^{2}\), respectively, in line with
the PCE values derived from the \( J-V \) curves. Figure 2d depicts the wavelength dependence of the external quantum efficiency (EQE) of these devices. The EQE values of the perovskite device on mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) are slightly higher than those of others cell, which is in good agreement with the observed trends in the photocurrent. To further study the role of a-\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) layer on device performance, planar PSCs based on compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (c-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)) and a-\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{c}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) were fabricated. Figure S7 shows the \( J-V \) curves of the corresponding devices with forward and backward scan directions. The summary of photovoltaic parameters is listed in Table S1. Form the result, it is clear that the a-\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) layer not only increases the \( V_{oc} \) and FF of the planar PSC device but also reduces its hysteresis index as compared to c-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) based planar PSC.
In order to rationalize the effect of the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) structure on the photovoltaic performance, we derived the band alignment of the perovskite with the EELs from ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) and UV-vis absorption measurements. Figure 3a shows the valence band and Fermi level of the EELs calculated from the UPS data. The valence band edges of mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC, and a-\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films are positioned at 7.68 eV, 8.22 eV, and 8.44 eV below vacuum, respectively. From the UV-visible data shown in Figure 3b, the band gaps of mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NCs, and a-\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) are 3.3 eV, 4.05 eV, and 4.1 eV, respectively. We used these values to derive the energy band diagrams plotted in Figure 3 for devices employing mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\)-NC, and mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) as EELs. Clearly, the conduction band edge of a-\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) on mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) is optimally positioned to facilitate the extraction of electrons from the perovskite and their subsequent injection into the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold, while the nano-crystalline layer of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) acts as a barrier explaining the poor fill factors produced with this architecture. The conduction band edge for the bare mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold is at 0.16 eV lower energy than that of the perovskite accounting
for the observed decrease in \( V_{oc} \) to an average of 1.08 V. This offset is greatly enhanced for the c-SnO\(_2\) overlayer, reducing the \( V_{oc} \) to below 1 V.
Figure S8 shows results from intensity modulated photovoltage spectroscopy (IMVS) measurements carried out at different open circuit voltages, which were adjusted by varying the light intensity. Each peak of the imaginary part of IMVS spectra can be associated to a time constant of the device under investigation. The time constants presented in panel (a) are in the 10–100 microsecond domain and are attributed to trapped carrier recombination. These recombination times are of similar magnitude for the two investigated types of PSCs, which employ EELs with and without a-SnO\(_2\) overlayers on the mp-TiO\(_2\) scaffold. Nevertheless, the values for bare mp-TiO\(_2\) show steeper voltage dependence than the ones obtained with a-SnO\(_2\)/mp-TiO\(_2\) double layer. The two curves reach a crossing point at ca 0.98 V, beyond which the carriers recombine more slowly for the a-SnO\(_2\) covered than for the bare TiO\(_2\) films.
The IMVS spectra shown in panel 6S (b) reveal the presence of a second time constant for the mp-TiO\(_2\) reference cell, which is much less pronounced for the mp-TiO\(_2\)/a-SnO\(_2\) one. This feature is attributed to the recombination of carriers across the perovskite/EEL interface being trapped in deeper states in the bulk of the electron transport layer increasing their lifetime to the hundred-millisecond domain. This slow relaxation process is likely to be coupled to the displacement of ions in the local electric field generated by the space charge of the trapped carriers. Even though we can observe the second peak at the low frequency also in the spectra of the double layer sample mp-TiO\(_2\)/a-SnO\(_2\), the signal is greatly reduced in magnitude and emerges only for very low illumination intensities. This shows that the amorphous SnO\(_2\) layer is capable of mitigate the deleterious effect of electron trapping states at the interface between the perovskite layer and the TiO\(_2\).
Moreover, the direct comparison of electroluminescence measurements confirmed the capability of the \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) treatment to suppress the nonradiative recombination losses (Figure 4). In fact, the external quantum efficiency (EQE) of mp- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sample at the same current density (16 \(\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) is higher than the standard mp- \(\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) structure, reaching approximately \(0.5\%\) in line with the higher \(V_{o c}\) values observed for the sample with \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) as EEL. Note that this value is commensurate with the EQE values achieved with state of the art perovskite devices.
Due to the photocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), the EEL using the mp- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold is prone to cause degradation of perovskite solar cells under UV light. Eliminating this unwanted effect is important for large-scale outdoor application of PSCs. Encouragingly, we observed that the device based on mp- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is more stable than mp- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) based solar cell under continuous UV light. For this experiment, the devices were exposed to a UV lamp and measured after each 3 hours. Figure 5 shows the stability of devices on mp- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and mp- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) after 60 hours exposure to the UV radiation. The PCE of perovskite solar cell based on mp- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}{-}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is maintains almost \(97\%\) of its initial value, while bare \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) shows \(18\%\) PCE loss under this condition. This clearly shows another advantage of our proposed double layers EEL for perovskite solar cell and forebodes well for the outdoor use of this device architecture.
## Conclusions
Our results show that applying a thin amorphous overlayer of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) scaffold as EEL in PSCs improves not only the power conversion efficiency but also enhances the UV stability of the device. A key observation of our study is that these benefits are specific to \(\mathsf{a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) and disappear upon its crystallization. The strong gain in the \(V_{o c}\) enabled by the amorphous \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) capping layer stands in stark contrast to the dramatic losses in the \(V_{o c}\) and PCE encountered with
crystalline \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films. This striking difference arises from larger band gap of amorphous layer and better band alignment with respect to triple cation perovskite compared to crystalline \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\). Based on the mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) architecture, we produce a PSC with a PCE of \(20.4\%\) which is \(6\%\) above that of a device using bare mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) as EEL. Importantly, it was discovered that the UV stability of perovskite device for double layer mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{a}-\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is improved drastically compared with pure mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) EEL, where after \(60\,\mathrm{h}\) exposure to UV lamp, the PCE loss is only \(3\%\).
